    "summary": "Epic Dark Star marathon with soaring Morning Dew and Bird Song",
    "blurb": "The June 10 1973 RFK show delivers a jaw‑dropping Dark Star/He's Gone/Wharf Rat/Truckin' trek, a soaring Morning Dew opener and a glittering Bird Song jam. Jerry’s guitar is on fire, Phil lays down a monster bass groove, and Keith adds lush texture throughout. It’s widely regarded as one of the most powerful three‑set outings of the era.",
    "review": "June 10, 1973 at RFK Stadium captured the Grateful Dead at a creative peak. The night opened with an electrifying “Morning Dew” that set a mystical tone, then rolled through classics like “Box of Rain,” “Bird Song,” and a blistering “Playing In The Band.” Set 2 hit its apex with a 26‑minute “Dark Star” that flowed straight into “He's Gone,” “Wharf Rat,” and “Truckin’, ” delivering the kind of marathon jam that still makes fans gasp. The third set, bolstered by an Allman Brothers guest appearance, kept the energy high with “Sugar Magnolia” and a fiery “Not Fade Away.” Across all sources the performance is described as “legendary,” “magical,” and “on fire,” while the soundboard transfers (especially Miller’s fix) are praised for crystal‑clear fidelity that lets every nuance shine.\n\nThe band was locked in: Jerry’s Chuck‑Berry‑style tone roared through Dark Star and Eyes Of The World, Phil’s bass was a “monster” foundation, Keith’s keyboards wove harmonic depth, and Bill’s jazz‑inflected drumming drove the jams forward. Even with a few weaker moments (the brief “Race Is On” and some uneven first‑set spots), the show’s highs far outweigh any dips, making this a must‑listen for anyone chasing that 1973‑summer Dead magic.",
